# {{.project_name}}

A judge experiment: grade your app's answers with an LLM judge, check first that the eval set can fail, keep every vote
on the trace, and send what the judge fails to a person. Made with `databricks bundle init` from the Daily Drill
template.

## What runs

One job, four notebooks on serverless compute. Each opens with what it does and why, and shows its results as tables
and plain text rather than raw output: open any task in the job run to read it.

| Notebook | What it does | Costs |
|---|---|---|
| `src/seed_sample.py` | Loads `fixtures/sample_eval_set.csv` into `eval_set`, only if there is no `eval_set` table yet | compute only |
| `src/check_set.py` | Reads reviewers' verdicts back as labels, adds synthetic failures, and **stops the job** if the set holds fewer than `min_failures` known-bad answers | compute only |
| `src/judge.py` | Code checks plus the judge (and any jury) as one MLflow evaluation run | model tokens |
| `src/report.py` | Catch and keep rates with 95% ranges, the jury against its best member, and a review queue for a person | compute only |

Every table lives in `{{.catalog}}.<schema>`. In the `dev` target the schema gets your user name, so two people never
overwrite each other.

## What MLflow records

The judge step creates the experiment with its traces in Unity Catalog (Delta tables in `{{.catalog}}.<schema>`), as
MLflow recommends on Databricks; it uses the `warehouse` SQL warehouse to set them up and read them. One evaluation run
then holds everything needed to trace a result back and repeat it:

| What | Where |
|---|---|
| The judge's instructions | Prompt `{{.catalog}}.<schema>.judge_instructions`, a new version only when `src/judge_instructions.md` changes; the run links the exact version, alias `in_use` |
| The eval set | Evaluation dataset `{{.catalog}}.<schema>.judge_eval_dataset`, linked to the run with its digest |
| Each answer | A trace with a `judged_answer` span (context, question, answer) and tags `row_id`, `group`, `planted`; the judge never sees those tags |
| Each verdict | Feedback on the trace from each judge, with its reason, tokens and cost; the code checks as CODE feedback |
| Who made each label | Planted mistakes as CODE (the plant), a person's verdict as HUMAN |
| Settings | Params: judge and jury models, prompt, dataset and digest, counts per planted kind, word limit, target |
| Spend | Metrics `<judge>_input_tokens`, `<judge>_output_tokens`, `<judge>_cost_usd`, `cost_usd` |
| Results | Metrics from the report (catch, keep, balanced per grader) and `report.json`, on the same run |

## A person in the loop

The report step puts every real answer the judge failed (or a jury split on) in a review queue in the experiment
(**Evaluation, Reviews**), with one question: *would you show this answer to a user?* A reviewer reads the context,
question and answer, chooses yes or no, and says why. The verdict is saved on the trace as HUMAN feedback, and the next
run's check step reads it back as the answer's label, so the judge is then measured against people. Review queues are a
Beta feature: a workspace admin turns on **MLflow Review Queues** in Manage previews. Without it the job still runs,
and the `review_queue` table lists the answers. Set the bundle variable `reviewers` to send the queue to other people.

## Run it

```bash
databricks bundle validate
databricks bundle deploy
databricks bundle run {{.project_name}}
```

Then open the experiment `/Users/<you>/{{.project_name}}-dev` in MLflow: the evaluation run lists every answer with each
judge's verdict and reason, and the report run holds `report.json`.

## Use your own answers

Create `{{.catalog}}.<schema>.eval_set` with these columns, before the first run (the sample only loads when there is no
`eval_set`):

| Column | Holds |
|---|---|
| `id` | A unique id per answer |
| `group` | A group to report by, such as a product area or question type |
| `context` | What the answer must stick to: the documents, the policy, the lesson |
| `question` | What was asked |
| `answer` | Your app's answer |
| `expected_pass` | `true` or `false` if a person has checked it, empty if nobody has |

Then change `src/judge_instructions.md` to your own rules, and `max_words` and the regexes in `code_checks` in
`src/judge_core.py` to your own limits.

## Why it's built this way

These choices come from an experiment on real tutor answers, where several judges were measured against planted
mistakes and labelled answers.

- **One strong judge, not a jury.** A jury of three did worse than its best member twice: judges that miss the same
  things can't vote them away (correlated errors), and two weak judges outvote a strong one. Set `jury_models` to
  measure a jury on your own answers; the report compares it with its best member and says whether the difference
  could be luck.
- **Rules go in code.** Word limits and phrases that lean on an earlier chat are checked by `code_checks`, so no judge
  is paid to count words. Judges are for meaning: whether the answer is correct and answers the question.
- **The set must be able to fail.** With almost no bad answers, every judge looks perfect and agreement is luck. The
  gate needs `min_failures` known-bad answers (96 by default: at a catch rate near a half, that gives a 95% range about
  10 points each way). Synthetic failures make up the gap until you have real ones; replace them as you collect real
  failures, because a planted mistake is usually plainer than a real one.
- **Labels can be wrong too.** In the sample, the first draft of the "good" answers added advice the context didn't
  support, and the judges were right to fail them. Read the `review_queue`, record a person's verdict in
  `expected_pass`, and the next run checks the judge against people, not against itself.
- **Same model, different settings, different judge.** Effort, prompt and endpoint all change verdicts. Log them, and
  compare runs only when they match.

## Read the report

- **catch**: of the known-bad answers, the share the judge failed. **keep**: of the known-good answers, the share it
  passed. A judge needs both; one that fails everything catches 100%.
- **balanced**: the two averaged. Empty when no answer is labelled good, because then the keep rate can't be measured.
- **by_kind**: catches per kind of planted mistake. A kind near 0 is a blind spot: fix the prompt, or move that rule
  into code.
- **jury_vs_best**: only with a jury. The answers only the jury got right against those only its best member got right,
  with a p-value; below 0.05 the difference is unlikely to be luck.

## Try it on your laptop first

With no Spark, the steps read and write CSV files in `.local/`, so you can try a cheap judge before spending in the
workspace:

```bash
cd src
python seed_sample.py && python check_set.py --min-failures 96 --experiment judge-local
python judge.py --judge-model anthropic:/claude-haiku-5-5 --experiment judge-local --max-words 200
python report.py --experiment judge-local
```

The notebooks are plain Python files in Databricks' notebook format, so they run as scripts too; tables print as
text. Without a catalog, the prompt and dataset get plain names in your local MLflow, traces stay in it, and review
queues are skipped.

This needs `mlflow` and `pandas`, a model key in the environment (here `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`), and `MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI`
pointing at an MLflow you can write to. A local MLflow server can run short of database connections when an evaluation
writes many traces at once; the judge step tries missing answers again, and stops if any never come back.

Run the logic's tests with `uv run --with pytest --with pandas pytest tests`.
